1
00:00:01,314 --> 00:00:06,500
Chapter 1. There was no possibility of taking a walk that day.

2
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:12,640
We had been wandering indeed in the leafless shrubbery and hour in the morning.

3
00:00:12,894 --> 00:00:18,240
But since dinner, Mrs. Reed, when there was no company, dined early.

4
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:24,840
The cold winter wind had brought with it cloud so somber and a rain so penetrating

5
00:00:25,439 --> 00:00:29,020
that further outdoor exercise was now out of the question.

6
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:31,679
I was glad of it.

7
00:00:32,439 --> 00:00:36,799
I never liked long walks, especially on chilly afternoons.

8
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:42,920
Dreadful to me was the coming home in the raw twilight, with nipped fingers and

9
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:49,500
toes, and a heart saddened by the chidings of Bessie, the nurse, and humbled by the

10
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:55,459
consciousness of my physical inferiority to Eliza, John, and Georgiana Reed.

11
00:00:56,650 --> 00:01:00,096
The said Eliza, John, and Georgiana, were now

12
00:01:00,097 --> 00:01:03,320
clustered around their mamma in the drawing-room.

13
00:01:03,930 --> 00:01:08,859
She lay reclined on a sofa by the fireside, and with her darlings about her,

14
00:01:09,409 --> 00:01:14,180
for the time neither quarreling nor crying, looked perfectly happy.

15
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:22,599
Me, she had dispensed from joining the group, saying, she regretted to be under

16
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:27,420
the necessity of keeping me at a distance, but that until she heard from Bessie,

17
00:01:27,939 --> 00:01:32,859
and could discover by her own observation that I was endeavouring in good earnest to

18
00:01:32,861 --> 00:01:37,900
acquire a more sociable and childlike disposition, a more attractive and

19
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:43,379
sprightly manner, something lighter, franker, more natural, as it were.

20
00:01:44,010 --> 00:01:49,939
She really must exclude me from privileges intended only for contented, happy,

21
00:01:50,299 --> 00:01:51,299
little children.

22
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:55,539
What does Bessie say I have done, I asked?

23
00:01:56,580 --> 00:01:59,939
Jane, I don't like Cavalars or questioners.

24
00:02:00,719 --> 00:02:03,769
Besides, there is something truly forbidding in

25
00:02:03,770 --> 00:02:06,080
a child, taking up her elders in that manner.

26
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:11,620
Be seated somewhere, and until you can speak pleasantly, remain silent.

27
00:02:13,469 --> 00:02:17,340
A breakfast room, a joined the drawing room, I slipped in there.

28
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:22,923
It contained a bookcase, I soon possessed myself of a volume,

29
00:02:22,924 --> 00:02:25,800
taking care that it should be one stored with pictures.

30
00:02:27,259 --> 00:02:32,439
I mounted into the window seat, gathering up my feet, I sat cross-legged

31
00:02:32,441 --> 00:02:38,819
like a turk, and having drawn the red marine curtain nearly close, I was shrined

32
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:44,819
in double retirement, folds of scarlet drapery shut in my view to the right hand,

33
00:02:45,639 --> 00:02:49,063
to the left with a clear panes of glass, protecting

34
00:02:49,064 --> 00:02:52,340
but not separating me from the drear November day.

35
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,925
At intervals, while turning over the leaves of my

36
00:02:57,925 --> 00:03:00,800
book, I studied the aspect of that winter afternoon.

37
00:03:02,344 --> 00:03:08,699
A far, it offered a pale blank of mist and cloud, near a scene of wet lawn and

38
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:12,010
stormbeat shrub, with ceaseless rains sweeping

39
00:03:12,012 --> 00:03:15,001
away wildly before a long and lamentable blast.

40
00:03:16,870 --> 00:03:22,719
I returned to my book, Buick's History of British Birds, the letterpress thereof I

41
00:03:22,721 --> 00:03:28,240
cared little for, generally speaking, and yet there were certain introductory

42
00:03:28,241 --> 00:03:33,639
pages that, child as I was, I could not pass quite as a blank.

43
00:03:35,009 --> 00:03:40,719
They were those which treat of the haunts of seafowl, of the solitary rocks and

44
00:03:40,721 --> 00:03:46,680
promontories by them only inhabited, of the coast of Norway, studded with ills

45
00:03:46,681 --> 00:03:51,840
from its southern extremity, the lindeness, or nays, to the north cape.

46
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:59,800
Where the northern ocean, in vast worlds, boils round the naked melancholy ills of

47
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:05,360
farthest thul, and the Atlantic surge pours in among the stormy hebrides.

48
00:04:06,970 --> 00:04:10,839
Nor could I pass unnoticed the suggestion of the bleak shores of Lapland,

49
00:04:11,699 --> 00:04:19,699
Siberia, Spitzbergen, Nova Zembla, Iceland, Greenland, with the vast sweep of

50
00:04:19,701 --> 00:04:25,779
the Arctic zone, and those forlorn regions of dreary space, that reservoir of frost

51
00:04:25,781 --> 00:04:31,199
and snow, where firm fields of ice, the accumulation of centuries of winters,

52
00:04:31,839 --> 00:04:35,396
glazed in alpine heights above heights, surround the

53
00:04:35,396 --> 00:04:39,959
pole, and consent the multiplied rigors of extreme cold.

54
00:04:41,649 --> 00:04:47,759
Of these death-white realms, I formed an idea of my own, shadowy, like all the half

55
00:04:47,761 --> 00:04:50,750
-comprehended notions that float dim through

56
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:54,060
children's brains, but strangely impressive.

57
00:04:55,149 --> 00:05:00,100
The words in these introductory pages connected themselves with the succeeding

58
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:05,699
vignettes, and gave significance to the rock standing up alone in a sea of billow

59
00:05:05,701 --> 00:05:12,180
and spray, to the broken boat, stranded on a desolate coast, to the cold

60
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:17,740
and ghastly moon glancing through bars of cloud, at a wreck just sinking.

61
00:05:19,050 --> 00:05:23,720
I cannot tell what sentiment haunted the quite solitary churchyard, with its

62
00:05:23,721 --> 00:05:28,740
inscribed headstone, its gate, its two trees, its low horizon,

63
00:05:29,319 --> 00:05:32,163
girdled by a broken wall, and its newly risen

64
00:05:32,165 --> 00:05:35,901
crescent, a testing the hour of even tide.

65
00:05:36,449 --> 00:05:42,160
The two ships become on a torpid sea, I believed to be marine phantoms.

66
00:05:43,019 --> 00:05:47,500
The fiend pinning down the thief's pack behind him, I passed over quickly.

67
00:05:48,240 --> 00:05:49,579
It was an object of terror.

68
00:05:51,639 --> 00:05:55,307
So was the black-horned thing seated aloof on a rock,

69
00:05:55,309 --> 00:05:58,579
surveying a distant crowd surrounding a gallows.

70
00:06:00,050 --> 00:06:02,420
Each picture told a story.

71
00:06:03,339 --> 00:06:08,160
Mysterious often to my undeveloped understanding and imperfect feelings,

72
00:06:08,709 --> 00:06:12,000
yet ever profoundly interesting.

73
00:06:12,949 --> 00:06:17,379
As interesting as the tails Bessie sometimes narrated on winter evenings,

74
00:06:18,050 --> 00:06:23,079
when she chanced to be in good humour, and when, having brought her ironing table

75
00:06:23,081 --> 00:06:27,000
to the nursery hearth, she allowed us to sit about it.

76
00:06:27,769 --> 00:06:33,019
And while she got up Mrs. Reed's lace frills, and crimped her night cat borders,

77
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:40,600
fed our eager attention with passages of love and adventure taken from old fairy

78
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:45,365
tales and other ballads, or, as at a later period I discovered,

79
00:06:45,365 --> 00:06:48,699
from the pages of Pamela and Henry, Earl of Moorland.

80
00:06:49,939 --> 00:06:55,180
With Buick on my knee, I was then happy, happy at least in my way.

81
00:06:56,310 --> 00:06:58,836
I feared nothing but interruption, and that

82
00:06:58,836 --> 00:07:01,940
came too soon, the breakfast room door opened.

83
00:07:03,634 --> 00:07:05,199
Boo, madam moep!

84
00:07:05,519 --> 00:07:07,939
cried the voice of John Reed, then he paused.

85
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:09,959
He found the room apparently empty.

86
00:07:11,069 --> 00:07:12,069
Where the Dickens is she?

87
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:13,800
He continued.

88
00:07:14,769 --> 00:07:17,100
Lizzie, Georgie, calling to his sisters.

89
00:07:18,079 --> 00:07:19,180
Joan is not here.

90
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:22,759
Tell Mama she has run out into the rain, bad animal.

91
00:07:24,689 --> 00:07:27,420
It is well I drew the curtain, thought I.

92
00:07:28,509 --> 00:07:31,600
And I wished fervently he might not discover my hiding place.

93
00:07:32,339 --> 00:07:34,879
Nor would John Reed have found it out himself.

94
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:40,779
He was not quick either a vision or conception, but Eliza just put her head in

95
00:07:40,781 --> 00:07:45,680
at the door and said at once, she is in the window seat to be sure Jack.

96
00:07:47,129 --> 00:07:50,153
And I came out immediately for I trembled at the

97
00:07:50,153 --> 00:07:53,500
idea of being dragged forth by the said Jack.

98
00:07:54,060 --> 00:07:55,060
What do you want?

99
00:07:55,610 --> 00:07:57,439
I asked with awkward dividends.

100
00:07:58,649 --> 00:08:02,819
Say, what do you want, master Reed, was the answer.

101
00:08:03,889 --> 00:08:05,180
I want you to come here.

102
00:08:06,310 --> 00:08:09,560
And seating himself in an armchair, he intimated by a

103
00:08:09,562 --> 00:08:12,920
gesture that I was to approach and stand before him.

104
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:19,480
John Reed was a schoolboy of 14 years old, four years older than I.

105
00:08:20,129 --> 00:08:21,259
For I was but ten.

106
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:25,639
Large and stout for his age with a dingy and unhulsome skin.

107
00:08:26,539 --> 00:08:32,019
Thick lanyaments in a spacious visage, heavy limbs and large extremities.

108
00:08:33,110 --> 00:08:37,046
He gorgeed himself habitually a table, which made him

109
00:08:37,047 --> 00:08:41,200
billious, and gave him a dim and blurred eye and flabby cheeks.

110
00:08:42,169 --> 00:08:43,820
He ought now to have been at school.

111
00:08:44,649 --> 00:08:50,821
But his Mama had taken him home for a month or two, on account of his delicate health.

112
00:08:51,570 --> 00:08:56,163
Mr. Miles, the master, affirmed that he would do very well

113
00:08:56,163 --> 00:08:59,639
if he had fewer cakes and sweet meats sent him from home.

114
00:09:01,049 --> 00:09:06,419
But the mother's heart turned from an opinion so harsh and inclined rather to

115
00:09:06,421 --> 00:09:10,548
the more refined idea that John's saloness was owing

116
00:09:10,548 --> 00:09:15,080
to overapplication and perhaps to pining after home.

117
00:09:16,230 --> 00:09:21,200
John had not much affection for his mother and sisters, and an antipathy to me.

118
00:09:22,399 --> 00:09:24,220
He bullied and punished me.

119
00:09:25,039 --> 00:09:29,879
Not two or three times in the week, nor once or twice in the day, but

120
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:34,043
continually, every nerve I had feared him, and every

121
00:09:34,044 --> 00:09:37,740
morsel of flesh in my bones shrank when he came near.

122
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:44,340
There were moments when I was bewildered by the terror he inspired, because I had

123
00:09:44,341 --> 00:09:49,080
no appeal whatever, against either his menaces or his inflicions.

124
00:09:50,210 --> 00:09:54,659
The servants did not like to offend their young master by taking my part against

125
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:58,240
him, and Mrs. Reed was blind and deaf on the subject.

126
00:09:58,990 --> 00:10:03,639
She never saw him strike or heard him abuse me, though he did both now and then,

127
00:10:04,259 --> 00:10:08,980
in her very presence, more frequently, however, behind her back.

128
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:13,559
Habitually, obedient to John, I came up to his chair.

129
00:10:14,580 --> 00:10:18,100
He spent some three minutes in thrusting out his tongue

130
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:20,860
at me as far as he could without damaging the roots.

131
00:10:21,909 --> 00:10:27,500
I knew he would soon strike, and while dreading the blow, I mused on the

132
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:31,320
disgusting and ugly appearance of him who would presently deal it.

133
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:38,159
I wonder if he read that notion in my face, for all at once, without speaking,

134
00:10:38,860 --> 00:10:40,860
he struck suddenly and strongly.

135
00:10:41,789 --> 00:10:44,727
I tottered, and on regaining my equilibrium

136
00:10:44,729 --> 00:10:47,500
retired back a step or two from his chair.

137
00:10:49,019 --> 00:10:55,340
That is for your impudence in answering Mama a while since, said he, and for your

138
00:10:55,341 --> 00:10:59,539
sneaking way of getting behind curtains, and for the look you had in your eyes,

139
00:10:59,860 --> 00:11:01,799
two minutes since you rat.

140
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:04,759
A custom to John Reed's abuse.

141
00:11:05,820 --> 00:11:08,039
I never had an idea of replying to it.

142
00:11:08,879 --> 00:11:12,779
My care was how to endure the blow which would certainly follow the insult.

143
00:11:14,179 --> 00:11:15,799
What were you doing behind the curtain?

144
00:11:16,639 --> 00:11:17,639
He asked.

145
00:11:17,899 --> 00:11:18,899
I was reading.

146
00:11:19,860 --> 00:11:20,860
Show the book.

147
00:11:21,919 --> 00:11:24,700
I returned to the window and fetched it thence.

148
00:11:25,970 --> 00:11:27,840
You have no business to take our books.

149
00:11:28,220 --> 00:11:30,019
You are a dependent, Mama says.

150
00:11:30,519 --> 00:11:31,320
You have no money.

151
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:32,919
Your father left you none.

152
00:11:33,519 --> 00:11:39,059
You ought to beg and not to live here with gentlemen's children like us, and eat the

153
00:11:39,061 --> 00:11:43,179
same meals we do, and wear clothes that our Mama's expense.

154
00:11:45,230 --> 00:11:50,120
Now, I'll teach you to rummage my bookshelves, for they are mine.

155
00:11:50,639 --> 00:11:54,700
All the house belongs to me, or will do in a few years.

156
00:11:55,465 --> 00:11:59,240
Go and stand by the door, out of the way of the mirror and the windows.

157
00:12:01,080 --> 00:12:07,659
I did so, not at first aware what was his intention, but when I saw him lift and

158
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:11,075
poise the book and stand in act to hurl it, I

159
00:12:11,076 --> 00:12:14,840
instinctively started aside with a cry of alarm.

160
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:22,480
Not soon enough, however, the volume was flung, it hit me, and I fell, striking my

161
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:27,059
head against the door and cutting it, the cut bled, the pain was sharp,

162
00:12:27,779 --> 00:12:31,679
my terror had passed its climax, other feelings succeeded.

163
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:35,799
Wicked and cruel boy, I said.

164
00:12:36,539 --> 00:12:38,591
You are like a murderer, you are like a slave

165
00:12:38,591 --> 00:12:41,280
driver, you are like the Roman emperors.

166
00:12:42,580 --> 00:12:45,456
I had read Goldsmith's history of Rome, and

167
00:12:45,456 --> 00:12:49,421
had formed my opinion of Nero Caligula and C.

168
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:53,100
Also, I had drawn parallels in silence, which

169
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:55,980
I never thought thus to have declared aloud.

170
00:12:56,669 --> 00:13:00,340
What, what, he cried, did you say that to me?

171
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:05,860
Did you hear her Eliza and Georgiana won't I tell Mama, but first?

172
00:13:06,919 --> 00:13:10,293
He ran head long at me, I felt him grasp my hair and

173
00:13:10,293 --> 00:13:12,879
my shoulder, he had closed with a desperate thing.

174
00:13:13,804 --> 00:13:16,860
I really saw in him a tyrant, a murderer.

175
00:13:18,190 --> 00:13:22,054
I felt a drop or two of blood from my head trickle down

176
00:13:22,056 --> 00:13:25,460
my neck, and was sensible of somewhat pungent suffering.

177
00:13:26,320 --> 00:13:28,979
These sensations for the time predominated

178
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:33,041
over fear, and I received him in frantic sort.

179
00:13:34,120 --> 00:13:38,759
I don't very well know what I did with my hands, but he called me rat, rat,

180
00:13:39,279 --> 00:13:40,419
and bellowed out aloud.

181
00:13:41,559 --> 00:13:46,820
Aid was near him, Eliza and Georgiana had run for Mrs. Reed, who was gone upstairs,

182
00:13:47,639 --> 00:13:51,580
she now came upon the scene, followed by Bessie and her maid Abbott.

183
00:13:52,879 --> 00:13:53,879
We were parted.

184
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:59,019
I heard the words, dear, dear, what a fury to fly at Master John.

185
00:14:00,620 --> 00:14:06,620
Did ever anybody see such a picture of passion, then Mrs. Reed sub-joined.

186
00:14:07,440 --> 00:14:09,480
Take her away to the red room and lock her in there.

187
00:14:11,039 --> 00:14:14,980
Four hands were immediately laid upon me, and I was born upstairs.
